1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,210
Every demo I've taken apart on this channel was made in Unity,
by people who write code. This one wasn't.

2
00:00:06,460 --> 00:00:13,167
It was made in a tool sold on the promise that you never have to program.
There's a C# compiler inside the build anyway.

3
00:00:17,017 --> 00:00:24,246
Veiled Shadows is a month in a rural mountain town.
You build bonds during the day, and at night you go into another world.

4
00:00:24,496 --> 00:00:31,177
Everyone who has played it reaches for the same word:
Persona. One reviewer counted the nods to Persona 4.

5
00:00:31,427 --> 00:00:37,115
The battle menu is drawn as a handheld console,
and your abilities are cartridges you slot into it.

6
00:00:37,365 --> 00:00:45,326
The demo went up five days ago. Six reviews, all positive.
That's not a reception, so what I can actually read here is the build.

7
00:00:45,576 --> 00:00:50,663
Steam publishes the file list for every demo.
This one has seventy one entries.

8
00:00:50,913 --> 00:01:01,199
The engine is RPG Developer Bakin, from a Japanese studio called SmileBoom.
Seventy dollars on Steam, and it's sold as programming-free RPG creation.

9
00:01:01,449 --> 00:01:06,719
The game's own executable is five hundred and sixty two kilobytes.
It's a launcher.

10
00:01:06,969 --> 00:01:13,493
What actually runs the game is bakinplayer.exe,
and the engine DLL sitting next to it.

11
00:01:13,743 --> 00:01:16,557
Then there's one file
that is the entire game.

12
00:01:16,807 --> 00:01:22,730
data.rbpack. One point three five gigabytes.
Ninety five percent of the download.

13
00:01:22,980 --> 00:01:29,504
Every map, every model,
every portrait and every voice line this person made is inside that one file.

14
00:01:29,754 --> 00:01:34,188
The other seventy entries are the engine,
shipped exactly as it arrived.

15
00:01:34,438 --> 00:01:39,865
Which is the interesting part,
because of what a no-code tool puts in your game without asking.

16
00:01:40,115 --> 00:01:49,094
First. Microsoft.CodeAnalysis, two DLLs,
ten point seven megabytes. That's Roslyn. That is a C# compiler.

17
00:01:49,344 --> 00:02:00,047
Bakin does offer an optional C# scripting feature, and this is what pays for it.
Every game made in the tool carries a compiler, whether its author wrote a line of C# or not.

18
00:02:00,297 --> 00:02:08,179
Second. WebView2, and an Edge browser control.
There is a web browser inside this turn-based RPG.

19
00:02:08,429 --> 00:02:14,901
I could not work out what it's for from a file list.
If you've built something in Bakin, the comments are right there.

20
00:02:15,151 --> 00:02:19,690
Third, and this is the one worth taking away.
VRM Loader.

21
00:02:19,940 --> 00:02:25,680
VRM is the avatar format VRoid Studio exports.
The format VTubers use.

22
00:02:25,930 --> 00:02:31,984
So a solo developer can pull a 3D character out of an
avatar maker instead of modelling one from scratch.

23
00:02:32,234 --> 00:02:40,116
The shaders ship as plain text, uncompiled. Seven include files,
thirty eight kilobytes between them. One is six bytes long.

24
00:02:40,366 --> 00:02:47,674
They get compiled on your machine when you play.
That's what the seventeen megabyte DirectX shader compiler next to them is for.

25
00:02:47,924 --> 00:02:55,153
And there's a log file in there. bakinplayer_log.txt,
from the developer's own session, shipped to players.

26
00:02:55,403 --> 00:03:04,356
It's fourteen kilobytes. Two episodes ago the same mistake was seven hundred and seventy megabytes of debug symbols.
Four orders of magnitude apart.

27
00:03:04,606 --> 00:03:16,798
There are four readme files too, in English, Japanese and both kinds of Chinese, on a store page that lists English only.
Those are the engine's own readmes, which tells you the folder went out untouched.

28
00:03:17,048 --> 00:03:21,639
Now the six reviews. You can't count six of anything,
but you can read them.

29
00:03:21,889 --> 00:03:28,517
One of them tells me the developer is one person in Austria.
That's the team size, from a player, not a press kit.

30
00:03:28,767 --> 00:03:35,630
Two of them mention the voice acting, unprompted.
And one had seven hours on a demo that covers a few in-game days.

31
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:38,224
Here's the whole teardown on one card.

32
00:03:38,474 --> 00:03:46,591
Bakin, seventy dollars, one person.
One point three five gigabytes of their content against sixty seven megabytes of engine they didn't write.

33
00:03:46,841 --> 00:03:49,655
Worth a dip,
and worth it for the art alone.

34
00:03:49,905 --> 00:03:57,970
If somebody has told you no-code means a low ceiling,
this build ships a C# compiler. The ceiling is wherever you stop.

35
00:03:58,220 --> 00:04:11,536
And before you publish, open your engine folder. That's my read from the outside, though. If you made this and I got something wrong,
say so and I'll pin the correction. Next time, another demo, another file list. This is Demo Dip.
